\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

We identify ten limitations that bound the scope of our conclusions.

\paragraph{Scale ceiling.}
All experiments use models at or below 14.7B parameters. Larger models (70B+) may exhibit qualitatively different contamination dynamics where SFT injection is absorbed without producing measurable signal divergence.

\paragraph{Single seed.}
The five-pattern taxonomy (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}) is based on seed$=$42 data. Triple-seed validation (s123, s0) is in progress to assess pattern stability with error bars.

\paragraph{SFT ecological validity.}
We use LoRA-based SFT injection exclusively in AUGMENT mode (contaminated items appended to, not replacing, base SFT data). Pretraining-stage leakage, which occurs during large-scale web crawl ingestion, may produce qualitatively different contamination signatures. The position bias at 0.5B (A-option rate escalating from 46\% to 98\% with dosage) is likely SFT-specific. Generalization to pretraining contamination requires further validation.

\paragraph{ARC-Challenge bootstrap stability.}
ARC-Challenge contains only 1{,}172 items (vs.\ 14{,}042 for MMLU), resulting in substantially lower bootstrap profile stability: OLMo-2 53.3\%, Gemma-2 62.7\% (vs.\ $\geq$76.6\% on MMLU). Gemma-2's ARC $\Delta$acc 95\% CI crosses zero, and Qwen-14B exhibits a different pattern (V-recovery) on ARC than on MMLU (U-collapse). Cross-benchmark consistency claims should be interpreted as directional agreement, not pattern-level replication.

\paragraph{Signal coverage.}
S1 (embedding similarity) requires training corpus access, unavailable for closed-source models. For API-only models, only S2 and S4 are applicable, and both show poor discrimination below 1.5B (\S\ref{sec:signal_failure}).

\paragraph{Ensemble size and per-domain power.}
Reliable $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$. Our domain-level analysis ($J = 13$--$16$) yields a strong directional signal for only one of eight MMLU domains. Per-domain $\gamma$ diagnosis at current ensemble sizes is exploratory.

\paragraph{Position bias.}
Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits position bias correlated with dosage (A-option rate: 46\% at d000 to 98\% at d100); these models are excluded from MC IRT analyses, reducing ensemble size.

\paragraph{Model misfit in non-STEM domains.}
A single linear $\gamma$ cannot capture non-monotone contamination effects within a domain. The five-pattern taxonomy operates at the model level; richer within-domain specifications may be required for domain-level calibration.

\paragraph{Additive $\gamma$ linearity.}
The 2PL+$\gamma$ model assumes contamination effects are additive and linear in exposure. PPP posterior predictive checks cannot detect threshold-type model misspecification (PPP remains 0.50--0.56 despite up to 33\% $\gamma$ overestimation), which highlights the value of simulation-based specification testing. The threshold DGP simulation uses uniform exposure; real RRF exposure is right-skewed (mean$=$0.041), so the quantitative overestimation magnitude may differ, but the qualitative direction (overestimation, not attenuation) is robust.

\paragraph{Prompt format sensitivity.}
The observed dose-response profiles are conditional on the evaluation protocol (5-shot prompting for MMLU, 25-shot for ARC-Challenge). Preliminary comparison with 0-shot evaluation suggests that pattern shapes may vary with prompt format---for instance, the V-type recovery pattern observed under 5-shot prompting can disappear under 0-shot conditions. This indicates that contamination effects interact with in-context learning mechanisms. While internal consistency is maintained (all 75 model--dosage configurations use the same protocol), practitioners replicating our analyses under different prompting regimes should expect quantitatively and potentially qualitatively different dose-response profiles. Future work should systematically evaluate protocol sensitivity across shot counts and prompt templates.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

We systematically profiled how SFT-injected contamination affects benchmark scores across model families and scales. Three findings emerge.

\paragraph{Five patterns, not one.}
The ``contamination inflates scores'' assumption is a gross oversimplification. Controlled injection across five models reveals five distinct dose-response patterns, from immunity to catastrophic collapse, governed by a baseline ability threshold at 64.7\% MMLU (95\% CI: 64.0--65.4\%). Models that lack the tested knowledge benefit from contamination (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp); models that already possess it are harmed (Qwen-14B $-$10pp) or immune (Phi-4). These patterns are directionally consistent across MMLU and ARC-Challenge, suggesting they reflect model-level properties rather than benchmark artifacts. Any calibration or mitigation strategy that assumes a uniform contamination direction will overcorrect for some models and undercorrect for others.

\paragraph{Why bigger $\neq$ more robust.}
The Qwen-14B model ($-$10pp peak) is 3$\times$ more fragile than Qwen-7B ($-$3pp), directly contradicting scale-robustness expectations. One hypothesis: SFT injection at higher-capability models disrupts more specialized internal representations (factual associations, reasoning chains) that smaller models have not yet developed. The 14B model has more to lose from the distributional shift introduced by contaminated fine-tuning data. This has practical implications: organizations fine-tuning frontier models on mixed-quality data face greater risk of capability degradation than those fine-tuning smaller models.

\paragraph{Diagnostic value beyond aggregate calibration.}
Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B) reveals additional heterogeneity: Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, while aggregate $\gamma \approx 0$ masks opposing domain-level effects. The IRT framework captures both the family-level taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) and domain-level decomposition (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), providing a richer characterization than any single aggregate metric. B6-DCR's overcorrection (MAE 0.108) illustrates the risk of assuming large uniform effects without measurement-theoretic grounding. Pretraining contamination may produce qualitatively different effects \citep{schaeffer2026causally}; scaling to 70B+ and testing naturally contaminated models are natural next steps.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cross-family results (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) use seed$=$42 data across five models from four independent families. Within-family IRT analyses (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}--\ref{sec:domain_differential}) use $J=16$ Qwen2.5 models (8$\times$1.5B + 8$\times$3B); domain-level contamination estimates (denoted $\gamma_d$, where $d$ indexes cognitive domains within a benchmark; \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}) are reported at $J=16$ in the text and $J=13$ in Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}.

\subsection{Five Dose-Response Patterns}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Five dose-response patterns under controlled SFT injection (seed$=$42). Baseline: d000 MMLU accuracy. $\Delta$acc: d000$\to$d100 change. A baseline threshold at 64.7\% (bootstrap 95\% CI: 64.0--65.4\%) separates models that benefit from contamination from those harmed or immune. Stab.: profile classification stability under item-level bootstrap ($B=10{,}000$).}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
Pattern & Model & Baseline & $\Delta$acc & Peak & Stab. \\
\midrule
Immune & Phi-4 14.7B & 76.4\% & $\pm$0.5pp & negl. & 100\% \\
U-collapse & Qwen-14B & 69.4\% & $-$9.4pp & $-$10.0\textsuperscript{d050} & 100\% \\
V-recovery & Qwen-7B & 66.1\% & $-$1.8pp & $-$3.1\textsuperscript{d025} & 99.6\% \\
Benefit & Gemma-2 9B & 61.9\% & $+$3.8pp & mono. & 76.6\% \\
Benefit-sat. & OLMo-2 7B & 53.8\% & $+$3.2pp & sat.\textsuperscript{d050} & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Controlled SFT injection across five models from four independent families reveals five distinct dose-response patterns (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), not a uniform inflation effect.

\paragraph{Baseline ability threshold.}
A threshold at 64.7\% MMLU baseline (bootstrap 95\% CI: 64.0--65.4\%, $B=10{,}000$) separates two regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:baseline_vs_effect}). Below it, OLMo-2 (53.8\%) gains $+$3.2pp (saturating at d050) and Gemma-2 (61.9\%) gains $+$3.8pp monotonically: contamination functions as knowledge injection for models that lack the tested knowledge. Above the threshold, Qwen-7B (66.1\%) drops $-$3.1pp at d025 before partial recovery, Qwen-14B (69.4\%) collapses by $-$10.0pp at d050, and Phi-4 (76.4\%) shows no measurable effect.

\paragraph{Counter-intuitive scale effect.}
Within the Qwen family, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B ($-$10.0pp vs.\ $-$3.1pp peak degradation). Higher baseline ability does not confer protection; SFT injection disrupts the 14B's more specialized representations more severely.

\paragraph{Cross-benchmark consistency.}
ARC-Challenge patterns are directionally consistent for four of five models: OLMo-2 benefits ($+$3.9pp), Qwen-7B ($-$3.8pp) and Qwen-14B ($-$3.4pp) are harmed, Phi-4 remains immune ($\pm$0.6pp). Gemma-2 ARC is excluded due to a 16pp evaluation artifact. However, ARC bootstrap stability is substantially lower than MMLU (OLMo-2: 53.3\%, Gemma-2: 62.7\%) due to the smaller item pool (1{,}172 vs.\ 14{,}042), and Gemma-2's ARC $\Delta$acc 95\% CI crosses zero ($[-2.47, +0.09]$pp). Qwen-14B exhibits V-recovery on ARC rather than U-collapse, indicating benchmark-specific pattern variation. Cross-benchmark consistency should therefore be interpreted as directional agreement at the coarse level (benefit vs.\ harm), not pattern-level identity.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
    \caption{MMLU (5-shot)}
    \label{fig:dose_response_mmlu}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_arc_dose_response.pdf}
    \caption{ARC-Challenge (25-shot)}
    \label{fig:dose_response_arc}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Accuracy vs.\ contamination dosage across five models from four families. Models below 64.7\% MMLU baseline benefit from contamination; models above are harmed or immune. Qwen-14B is 3$\times$ more fragile than Qwen-7B. ARC patterns are directionally consistent for four of five models.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

Within-family IRT analysis uses Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B ($J=16$), where aggregate contamination shifts accuracy by ${\sim}$0.6--0.7pp. The taxonomy above (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) shows this near-null aggregate is specific to the $\leq$3B scale band; other families exhibit effects from $-$10pp to $+$4pp.

\paragraph{Item filtering improves calibration.}
RRF-guided item filtering achieves $\tau = 0.73$ on 300 items ($\Delta\tau = 0.27$ vs.\ random 2000-item baseline). S1-only selection causes convergence failure ($\hat{R} = 1.53$, ESS$=$7) due to exposure variance compression; multi-signal RRF fusion preserves broader distributions necessary for $\gamma$ identifiability ($\Delta\tau = 0.177$ vs.\ size-matched random).

\paragraph{Calibration comparison.}
Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods on 300 RRF-filtered items. Raw accuracy achieves the lowest MAE (0.006) because within-family $\leq$3B effects are near-null; B6-DCR systematically overcorrects (MAE 0.108). The IRT model differentiates ability across scales ($\Delta\theta = 0.50$, 95\% CI $[0.43, 0.57]$; Appendix~\ref{sec:theta_separation}). ContamCal's value is diagnostic ($\gamma$ CI), not calibration improvement.

\paragraph{Diagnostic value of $\gamma$.}
Although $\gamma$ does not improve aggregate calibration within this scale band, domain analysis reveals heterogeneity: Social Sciences $\gamma_d = -0.63$ with posterior $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma}, \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}). Replacing RRF scores with ground-truth labels, pooled $\gamma$ remains non-significant ($\gamma = -0.012$, CI $[-0.145, 0.119]$), consistent with opposing domain-level effects canceling at aggregate. Threshold-type misspecification is ruled out by simulation (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold_dgp}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU calibration on 300 RRF-filtered items (Qwen2.5, $J$=16). Raw accuracy achieves lowest MAE because within-family $\leq$3B effects are near-null. ARC results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & MAE & $\tau$ \\
\midrule
B0: Raw Accuracy & \textbf{0.006} & 0.767 \\
B1b: Remove \& Rescore & 0.026 & \underline{0.783} \\
B3: Vanilla 2PL & 0.008 & 0.731 \\
B4: Linear Regression & 0.010 & 0.751 \\
B5b: Emperor & \textbf{0.006} & \textbf{0.789} \\
B6: DCR (reimpl.) & 0.108 & 0.654 \\
ContamCal (2PL+$\gamma$) & 0.009 & 0.731 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits a \textit{directional reversal} at the 0.5B scale: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores than clean items (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ at d005, $-1.98$ at d100). S1 (embedding similarity) achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at all scales; S4 (Self-Critique) is near-random below 1.5B (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap} in Appendix). Multi-signal RRF fusion is essential: S1-only selection causes convergence failure due to exposure variance compression (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}).

\subsection{Identifiability Boundaries}
\label{sec:identifiability}

Simulation (200 runs; Figure~\ref{fig:identifiability_heatmap} in Appendix) shows $\gamma$ recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$ (bias $< 0.05$, coverage $\geq 90\%$); CI width scales as $J^{-0.48}$. At AUC $\geq 0.90$ (as S1 achieves), $J = 16$ suffices. Current practice ($J = 8$--$30$; \citealp{habba_2025_growing_pains,lalor_2024_irt_nlp}) operates near this threshold.

\subsection{Domain-Level Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5, $J=16$) complements the across-family taxonomy of \S\ref{sec:taxonomy}: contamination effects are heterogeneous at both the model level and the domain level. STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Social Sciences is negative ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, 95\% CI $[-1.07, -0.19]$, $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, PPP$=$0.722), crossing the Bonferroni-corrected threshold for eight tests (Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}, Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} in Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_gamma_table}). The pooled $\gamma_{\text{pooled}} = +0.11$ (CI $[-0.40, +0.63]$) is non-significant given opposing domain signs. Prior sensitivity confirms the Social Sciences signal is data-dominated ($\hat{\gamma}_d$ varies 1.2$\times$ across priors vs.\ 2.6$\times$ for pooled; Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity}). The contrast between modest $\leq$3B within-family effects and the dramatic cross-family patterns in \S\ref{sec:taxonomy} underscores that contamination susceptibility is model-dependent, not a fixed benchmark property.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Controlled SFT Injection}
\label{sec:injection}

We construct a controlled contamination testbed spanning four independent model families from four labs, trained on independent corpora with distinct pre-training recipes: Qwen2.5 \citep{qwen2024qwen25} (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B, 14B), Phi-4 \citep{abdin2024phi4} (14.7B), OLMo-2 \citep{olmo2team2025} (7B), and Gemma-2 \citep{gemmateam2024gemma2} (9B). All models are fine-tuned with a unified LoRA configuration \citep{hu2022lora} ($r=16$, $\alpha=32$, lr$=5\times10^{-5}$, bf16) on a general-purpose SFT corpus into which benchmark items are injected at five dosage levels: d000 (0\%, clean control), d005 (5\%), d025 (25\%), d050 (50\%), and d100 (100\%). Contaminated items augment (not replace) the base SFT corpus; at d100, 16{,}533 benchmark items are added beyond 50{,}000 base examples.

Qwen2.5 serves as the primary within-family series (five scale points, 0.5B--14B). Models at $\leq$3B are trained with two seeds at four dosage levels; 7B and 14B at all five dosages. The three cross-family models are each trained at five dosages with a single seed, serving as consistency checks rather than independent confirmatory analyses. This yields over 50 checkpoints spanning eight sizes across four families. We exclude 0.5B from MC IRT due to position bias (A-option rate: 46\% at d000 to 98\% at d100; \S\ref{sec:signal_failure}).

\subsection{Benchmarks, Domains, and Evaluation}
\label{sec:benchmarks}

We evaluate on three benchmarks spanning distinct cognitive demands and response formats.

\textbf{MMLU} \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu} (5-shot): 14,042 multiple-choice items across 57 subjects, grouped into 8 cognitive domains (Math, Science, CS \& Engineering, Medical, Humanities, Social Sciences, Business \& Law, Other). This stratification reveals within-benchmark heterogeneity that aggregate scoring obscures (\S\ref{sec:results}).

\textbf{ARC-Challenge} \citep{clark2018arc} (25-shot): 1,172 multiple-choice science reasoning items.

\textbf{GSM8K} \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}: 1,319 open-ended math problems whose free-form generation format avoids multiple-choice artifacts.

All multiple-choice evaluations use logprob-based scoring under an aligned instruction-template format with fixed few-shot counts; all 75 model--dosage configurations are evaluated under this identical protocol.

We assess calibration quality via MAE (calibrated vs.\ ground-truth clean accuracy), Kendall $\tau$ (rank preservation), credible interval coverage, posterior predictive $p$-value (PPP), $\gamma$ direction ($P(\gamma\!<\!0 \mid \text{data})$), and split-$\hat{R}$ convergence.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

We compare against seven baselines: \textbf{B0-Raw} (no adjustment), \textbf{B1-Remove\&Rescore} (drop flagged items; B1b uses RRF-filtered items), \textbf{B2-Single2PL+$\gamma$} (aggregate $\gamma$ without domain stratification), \textbf{B3-Vanilla2PL} ($\gamma=0$, isolating IRT ability estimation), \textbf{B4-LinearRegression} (residuals on exposure scores), \textbf{B5-Emperor} (mitigation strategies from \citet{sun2025emperor}; B5b uses the best-performing variant), and \textbf{B6-DCR} (our reimplementation following \citealp{xu2025dcr}).

